From: Jesse Osiecki <jesse@jjo.ninja>
Subject: [PATCH] greeter: let startAuthenticating() re-arm finished non-interactive authenticators

startAuthenticating() returns early while the state is Authenticating, and
the state only goes back to Idle when the interactive (password)
conversation fails or succeeds. A non-interactive authenticator that ends
on its own, such as a face-recognition module in the kde-fingerprint slot
that gives up after its timeout, therefore stays dead until the user types
a wrong password. fprintd never showed this because its conversation waits
indefinitely. Restart the non-interactive authenticators on every
startAuthenticating() call.

For that to be safe, PamAuthenticator::tryUnlock() must ignore the call
while a conversation is running: PamWorker::authenticate() is invoked
through the worker thread's event loop, so its own in-progress guard never
sees the second call; it is queued and starts one more conversation as
soon as the current one ends (visible today as an extra run after the
grace timer fires during a run).

Signed-off-by: Jesse Osiecki <jesse@jjo.ninja>
---
--- a/greeter/pamauthenticator.cpp
+++ b/greeter/pamauthenticator.cpp
@@ -342,6 +342,13 @@
 
 void PamAuthenticator::tryUnlock()
 {
+    if (m_inAuthentication) {
+        // A conversation is already running. authenticate() is invoked
+        // through the worker's event loop, so a second call would not be
+        // rejected by its guard but queued, and start another conversation
+        // the moment this one ends.
+        return;
+    }
     m_unlocked = false;
     QMetaObject::invokeMethod(d, &PamWorker::authenticate);
 }
--- a/greeter/pamauthenticators.cpp
+++ b/greeter/pamauthenticators.cpp
@@ -141,7 +141,19 @@
 
 void PamAuthenticators::startAuthenticating()
 {
-    if (d->state == AuthenticatorsState::Authenticating || d->graceLocked) {
+    if (d->graceLocked) {
+        return;
+    }
+
+    if (d->state == AuthenticatorsState::Authenticating) {
+        // The interactive conversation is still waiting for input, but a
+        // non-interactive one may have ended on its own (a face module gives
+        // up after a few seconds, unlike fprintd). Start those again so the
+        // lock screen can re-arm them on wake; one that is still running is
+        // left alone, tryUnlock() is a no-op while busy.
+        for (auto &&noninteractive : d->noninteractive) {
+            noninteractive->tryUnlock();
+        }
         return;
     }
 
